  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 153 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nWednesday through Thursday night\nAssessment...a certainty of cooler and less humid conditions\n\nWednesday and Thursday will be banner days with cooler temperatures \nand dew points in the 50s.\n\nThe models have trended stronger with the high over the western \nGreat Lakes which has now resulted in Thursday night being dry. This \nstronger high has ramifications for rain chances Friday\n\nFriday\nAssessment...low (<20%) confidence on rain chances\n\nThe global models have now confined any rain chances on Friday to \nthe morning hours. Dprog/Dt shows the system further south than \nyesterday which is a result of the high in the western Great Lakes \nbeing stronger.\n\nThe deterministic model runs (save the GFS) are dry for Friday. \nHowever, there are some ensemble members from each global model that \nhave rain chances across the area Friday morning. \n\nWith the high stronger and the system further south in Missouri, the \nprospects for rain are not good. A review of the Integrated Vapor \nTransport shows the strongest values run from Oklahoma into \nsoutheast Kansas and southern Missouri. Thus while the model \nconsensus currently has 15-35 percent rain chances Friday morning, I \nsuspect these values will slowly drop over the next two days.\n\nFriday night through Monday\nAssessment...medium to high (60-80%) confidence of rising humidity\nlevels\n\nReturn flow develops across the Midwest starting Friday night and \ncontinues through the weekend as the heat dome builds north into the \nPlains. Dew points will slowly increase back into the low to mid 60s \non Saturday with mid and upper 60 dew points Sunday into Monday. The \ncombination of rising temperatures and dew points will eventually \npush heat indices into the 90s again.",
